1. LICENSE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This is a **dual-licensed repository**. The two layers carry different
licenses, and the boundary is the provenance boundary itself:

  * **Qianmo's own code -- GNU AGPL-3.0-or-later** (`LICENSE` at the root).
    This covers the `@qianmo/*` workspace packages under `packages/`, the
    residency modifications to the base runtime, the demo and acceptance
    suites under `demo/`, and documentation written by the Qianmo AgentNest
    Team (`docs/dev/` and others). The test is the **file header**: every file
    in this layer carries both `SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later`
    and `Copyright 2026 Qianmo AgentNest Team` -- 580 files as of 2026-08-30.
    The count grows whenever a file with that header is added, so the command
    below, not this number, is what to rely on.

  * **Code imported from the base project -- MIT License** (`LICENSE.base`,
    the upstream text kept verbatim). Base files carry **no SPDX header**,
    which is the machine-checkable test between the two layers:

        # the files in the AGPL layer -- 580 of them as of 2026-08-30
        # (restricted to the file header, so documents that merely quote
        # the identifier -- this file among them -- are not counted)
        git grep -n "SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later" \
          | awk -F: '$2<=5' | cut -d: -f1 | sort -u

    See section 2 below.

  * **The `vendor/audio-capture/` exception no longer exists, but which layer
    those files sit in has to be stated separately**: the six prebuilt `.node`
    files there previously had no source at all anywhere. They have now all
    been rebuilt from source held in this repository
    (`packages/audio-capture-napi/native/`, whose source files all carry the
    AGPL-3.0-or-later file header): `arm64-darwin`, `x64-darwin`,
    `arm64-linux` and `x64-linux` were replaced on 2026-08-30, and `x64-win32`
    and `arm64-win32` were built the same day on a GitHub Actions
    `windows-latest` runner and installed. **They belong to the AGPL layer, but
    not by virtue of a file header — they belong to it because they are build
    artifacts of that AGPL source**: derived from it and distributed together
    with it, they carry the license of the source they are built from, and the
    Corresponding Source for them is `packages/audio-capture-napi/native/` in
    this very repository.

    Two points must be read alongside that, so the test in the previous bullet
    is not misapplied to them:

      - The `git grep … | awk -F: '$2<=5'` command above is **a test over
        source files** — it reads file headers — and a binary is outside its
        scope by construction. These six `.node` files are therefore **not
        part of that count** — the 580 files it reports are all source files.
        Running the command simply never lists them, and that is not an
        omission.
      - The reverse inference "carries no SPDX header, therefore it is in the
        base MIT layer" **does not hold for binaries**. The previous bullet
        describes how the base and Qianmo layers of **source files** are told
        apart; it is not a rule for classifying build artifacts shipped with
        the repository.

    **A separate verification gap remains**: `arm64-win32` has never been
    loaded or executed on any machine (the runner that built it is x86_64 and
    cannot load an aarch64 DLL), so its evidence is limited to a successful
    build, a clean `clippy -- -D warnings`, and structural checks. **That is a
    verification gap, not a license gap** — it comes from the same source as
    the other five and nothing about its license is undetermined. The
    per-triple level of evidence is set out in section 5 below.

**Why AGPL rather than GPL**: Qianmo's second product line is a communication
and collaboration network connecting resident agents. The most likely way for
it to be taken without reciprocity is for someone to run it as a hosted
service -- which GPL-3.0 does not reach and AGPL-3.0 section 13 (Remote
Network Interaction) does. For a network-shaped product, only AGPL is strict
in substance.

**The MIT-to-AGPL direction is permitted**: MIT is permissive and may be
incorporated into an AGPL work; the reverse is not true. Contributing this
repository's changes to the base layer back upstream (MIT) therefore requires
a separate grant. The base project and this repository share one owner, so
dual licensing is available, but it is **not automatic** and is decided case
by case. Per roadmap v2.41 the current position on upstreaming is "not for
now, revisit individually".

**This change is not retroactive**: the repository was distributed under MIT
before this change. Copies obtained under MIT keep that grant; AGPL applies
only to versions published after this change.

**Section 13 binds us too**: AGPL-3.0 section 13 requires an operator to offer
Corresponding Source to users who interact with the program over a network.
Qianmo's console and its resident nodes are exactly that, **including the
deployments we run ourselves**. This is the one ongoing obligation the
relicensing adds, and it is not a clause that only applies to other people.

  Note: earlier revisions of this repository (commit 67f6081 and prior) used the
  Apache License 2.0. That decision was superseded by the base-adoption change;
  see docs/dev/charter.md §5.5.

