5. THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This repository includes third-party components introduced by the base project
and its dependency tree, each under its own license.

**Dependency and license inventory (SBOM)**: the machine-readable inventory is
docs/dev/sbom-m0.json and the human-readable summary is docs/dev/sbom-m0.md.
Both are generated by `bun run sbom` and can be reproduced at any time.

**Prebuilt native binaries shipped with the repository** (not from the npm
dependency tree):

  * `vendor/audio-capture/` — `audio-capture.node` (a native N-API addon) for
    six platform triples (arm64/x64 × darwin/linux/win32), imported together
    with the base snapshot and copied into `dist/vendor/` by `build.ts` /
    `scripts/post-build.ts`, i.e. shipped with the build output. **The fact
    that no source existed anywhere has been resolved**:
    `packages/audio-capture-napi/native/` (Rust + napi-rs 2.16 / cpal 0.15.3)
    has been checked in as a from-scratch rewrite (clean-room, not decompiled
    from the original binaries). Its source files all carry the
    `SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later` header and implement all
    eight exports the loader at `packages/audio-capture-napi/src/index.ts`
    contracts for (`startRecording` / `stopRecording` / `isRecording` /
    `startPlayback` / `writePlaybackData` / `stopPlayback` / `isPlaying` /
    `microphoneAuthorizationStatus`). The crate requires Rust >= 1.85
    (`coreaudio-sys` needs edition2024), pinned to 1.85.0 via the crate's own
    `rust-toolchain.toml`. Build and verification steps are in
    docs/dev/audio-capture-native.md.

    **Replacement progress per triple (stated plainly: 6/6 done; the level of
    evidence differs — `arm64-win32` has build and structural checks only, no
    runtime evidence)**:
      - `arm64-darwin` — **replaced**: built from scratch successfully,
        `clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` and the unit tests pass; verified
        on real local hardware via Bun for both recording (16 kHz / 16-bit LE
        / mono, 50 ms chunks, consistent across three runs at 15584-15600 Hz)
        and playback (start/stop, idempotency, restart), all eight exports are
        present, and `microphoneAuthorizationStatus()` returns 3, matching
        both the original vendor binary and an independent Swift probe.
      - `x64-darwin` — **replaced**: verified via **Rosetta 2 with a real
        x86_64 Node v26.3.0**, loaded side by side with the original vendor
        binary — all eight exports present, `microphoneAuthorizationStatus()`
        returns 3, and status-flag behaviour matches.
      - `x64-linux` — **replaced**: verified via **Docker `linux/amd64` with
        `node:22-slim`** (with `libasound2` installed), loaded side by side
        with the original vendor binary — matches item-for-item.
      - `arm64-linux` — **replaced**: verified via a **native Docker
        `linux/arm64` container**, loaded side by side with the original
        vendor binary — matches item-for-item.
      - `x64-win32` — **replaced**: built by the manually triggered workflow
        `.github/workflows/build-audio-capture-windows.yml` (`workflow_dispatch`
        only, `windows-latest`, target `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`) and loaded for
        real with Node on that same runner — all eight exports are functions
        and `microphoneAuthorizationStatus()` returns 3 (within the valid range
        0..=3).
      - `arm64-win32` — **replaced, but with no runtime evidence**: built by
        the `aarch64-pc-windows-msvc` matrix leg of the same workflow;
        `cargo build --release` succeeded and
        `cargo clippy --release -- -D warnings` is clean. **This artifact has
        never been loaded or executed on any machine** — the `windows-latest`
        runner is x86_64 and structurally cannot load an aarch64 DLL. The
        workflow does not pretend otherwise: it prints an explicit skip reason
        instead. Beyond the build and the static check, the only evidence is
        structural (PE machine type `0xaa64`, `napi_register_module_v1`
        present, all eight export-name strings present, dependency surface
        identical to the original). **A load check on a real Windows on ARM
        machine is still owed.** This is a verification gap, not a license
        gap — the artifact comes from the same source as the other five.

    **On `--allow-unverified-install` (to avoid the misreading that these were
    installed without verification) — this paragraph is about the three
    cross-architecture darwin/linux artifacts only; the two Windows artifacts
    are covered immediately below**: the automatic gate in
    `scripts/build-audio-capture.sh` can only verify whether *this* machine
    can natively `require()`-load an artifact, so `x64-darwin`, `arm64-linux`
    and `x64-linux` went through that flag. **That does not mean they were
    unverified** — each was loaded for real on its target architecture (a
    container for the two Linux triples, Rosetta 2 for `x64-darwin`) and
    compared side by side against the original vendor binary before being
    installed by hand. That is a different thing from installing something
    that was never tested at all.

    **The two Windows artifacts did not go through that script at all, so the
    sentence above does not cover them**: `scripts/build-audio-capture.sh
    --install` copies the path cargo has **just built on this machine**
    (`built_path`), and cannot install a file downloaded from elsewhere. What
    was actually done: `gh run download` fetched the two artifacts from
    workflow run `33292271322`; after checking the PE machine type,
    `napi_register_module_v1`, the eight export names and the dependency
    surface, they were **copied directly into**
    `vendor/audio-capture/{x64-win32,arm64-win32}/audio-capture.node`, with the
    originals backed up first (and retrievable via `git show 5459375f:…`, see
    below). The level of evidence diverges at this point:
      - `x64-win32` — **had runtime verification before it was installed**:
        the same runner that built it loaded it for real with Node in the same
        run; all eight exports were functions and
        `microphoneAuthorizationStatus()` returned 3.
      - `arm64-win32` — **is the one triple of the six for which "no runtime
        verification at the time it was installed" is literally true**. The
        defence made just above for the three cross-architecture artifacts
        ("that does not mean they were unverified") **does not apply to it**,
        and the two must not be read as one: those three were loaded for real
        on their target architecture before being installed, whereas this one
        has still never been loaded or executed on any machine.

    **One piece of runtime-dependency equivalence evidence (answering "does
    swapping in the self-built artifact break things for Linux users?")**:
    comparing the `NEEDED` section via `readelf -d` on the Linux artifacts —
    the original vendor binary depends on `libasound.so.2`, `libpthread.so.0`,
    `libc.so.6`, `libdl.so.2`, `ld-linux-x86-64.so.2`; the self-built artifact
    depends on all of those **plus** `libgcc_s.so.1` and `libm.so.6`.
    **Conclusion: the hard dependency on ALSA was already there in the
    original vendor binary, not introduced by the self-built one**; the two
    extra libraries are present on any glibc system as a matter of course. The
    runtime dependency surface has not materially changed.

    **The same evidence for the Windows artifacts, and there it is a zero
    difference**: extracting the referenced DLL names
    (`strings -a <file> | grep -oiE '[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+\.dll'`, excluding the
    crate's own module name) and comparing them item by item — the original
    and the self-built artifact reference **the same 8 libraries, with no
    difference at all**: `advapi32.dll`, `api-ms-win-core-synch-l1-2-0.dll`,
    `bcryptprimitives.dll`, `dbghelp.dll`, `kernel32.dll`, `ntdll.dll`,
    `ole32.dll`, `oleaut32.dll`. **This is stronger evidence than the Linux
    comparison above**, where the self-built artifact added two libraries.
    It did not come out that way on the first attempt: the first Windows build
    additionally pulled in `vcruntime140.dll`, which ships with neither Windows
    itself nor the Node.js installer, so on a machine without the Visual C++
    redistributable `require()` would throw — and the loader in
    `packages/audio-capture-napi/src/index.ts` swallows that error and degrades
    silently, so the symptom would have been voice mode disappearing without a
    trace. Building with `-C target-feature=+crt-static` (set per target, not
    globally) removed it; the reasoning and the check are in
    docs/dev/audio-capture-native.md section 3.2.

    **The present state is therefore**: all six binaries under
    `vendor/audio-capture/` **are built from source held in this repository**;
    the original vendor artifacts brought in with the base snapshot have all
    been replaced, and no binary of undetermined license remains in the
    repository. **The provenance of the originals is kept here for the
    record**: the upstream repository
    (https://github.com/sweetcornna/open-claude-code) likewise carries only
    those six prebuilt artifacts, with no accompanying source, build script or
    LICENSE file (checked 2026-08-15); symbols in those binaries indicate a
    Rust + napi-rs build (statically linked napi 2.16.17, cpal 0.15.3, and per
    platform coreaudio-rs 0.11.3 / alsa 0.9.1 / windows 0.54.0). The two
    replaced Windows originals can still be retrieved with
    `git show 5459375f:vendor/audio-capture/<triple>/audio-capture.node`
    (sha256 `2a8267f4…` for x64, `98386089…` for arm64).

    **How this interacts with the relicensing to AGPL (recorded plainly)**: the
    project owner had decided to bring these six binaries into the copyleft
    layer and to supply their source — the source has been supplied, and
    **all six triples are now artifacts built from it**, so the obstacle to
    labelling them AGPL ("labelled AGPL but no source to ship") no longer
    exists. Of the original two ways to close this, the project owner chose
    the first — supply source and mark as AGPL — and that work is now done.
    **What remains is a verification gap, not a license gap**: `arm64-win32`
    has no runtime evidence and needs one load check on a real Windows on ARM
    machine. Its license standing is not in question — it comes from the same
    in-repository AGPL source as the other five. Tracked as D-9 (disposed) in
    docs/dev/license-chain-m0.md.

